{0 No future}

Nofuture is a unified monadic API for OCaml concurrency libraries such
as {!Async}, {!Eio} and {!Lwt}.

{1 Reference}

The entry point of the main library is the module {!Nofuture}.

{1 Rationale}

{1 Limitation and concerns}

{1 Quickstart}

{2 Blocking computations}

The core primitive of nofuture is the [Blocking] monad which
represents a computation that can block on external dependencies, such
as waiting for network i/o or acquiring a mutex. Its goal is to ensure
other blocking computation can run in the meantime when this happens.

{@ocaml toplevel[
# (* This execution thread will block for one second, but other one
  may execute in the meantime.  *)
  run @@ fun () ->
  let+ () = sleep 1. in
  print_endline "J'ai bien dormi"
+mock time is now 1
J'ai bien dormi

- : unit = ()
]}

Combining blocking computations with eg. {!Blocking.both} or
{!Blocking.all} states the combined computations are independent and
{i CAN} be run concurrently, but depending on the backend it doesn't
mean they {i WILL} be run concurrently.

For instance, the {!Nofuture_lwt} backend will run the jobs
concurrently:

{@ocaml toplevel[
# open Nofuture_lwt
# run @@ fun () ->
  let+ () =
    let+ () = sleep 3. in
    print_endline "Je me croyais au paradis"
  and+ () =
    let+ () = sleep 2. in
    print_endline "Et moi aussi"
  and+ () =
    let+ () = sleep 1. in
    print_endline "J'ai bien dormi"
  in
  ()
J'ai bien dormi
Et moi aussi
Je me croyais au paradis

- : unit = ()
]}

The {!Nofuture_eio} backend will however run them sequentially:

{@ocaml toplevel[
# open Nofuture_eio
# run @@ fun () ->
  let+ () =
    let+ () = sleep 3. in
    print_endline "Je me croyais au paradis"
  and+ () =
    let+ () = sleep 2. in
    print_endline "Et moi aussi"
  and+ () =
    let+ () = sleep 1. in
    print_endline "J'ai bien dormi"
  in
  ()
+mock time is now 3
Je me croyais au paradis
+mock time is now 5
Et moi aussi
+mock time is now 6
J'ai bien dormi

- : unit = ()
]}

This is because {!Blocking} is not a concurrency primitive per se, but
rather a plumbing primitive that gives the concurrency backend the
opportunity to do its deed and not block the whole domain. You may see
it as the tax you must pay to make your concurrent code work on all
backends. While combiners are not guaranteed to produce concurrency it
is still a good idea to use them when possible as it's always better to
not introduce unneccessary sequential dependencies.

Similarly, exception raised from a {!Blocking} computation must be
caught using {!Blocking.catch} as they may escape the standard [try]
keyword.

{@ocaml toplevel[
# run @@ fun () ->
  catch
    (fun () ->
       let+ () = yield () in
       raise End_of_file)
    (function
     | End_of_file -> print_string "EOF"
     | e -> raise e)
EOF
- : unit = ()
]}

